1
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,480
CONVERSATION 1
Thanks for inviting me out tonight.

2
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:41,660
I've been wanting to try this place for weeks.

3
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:46,920
I usually pass it on my way home from work but never seem to have time to stop.

4
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:49,120
I've been dying to come here as well.

5
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:52,100
I was worried that the menu might not be accommodating.

6
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:55,000
But one of my close friends ate here last week.

7
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:55,600
She's a vegetarian.

8
00:00:55,600 --> 00:01:02,080
She told me that there were a lot of options for her, and they offered alternative dishes.

9
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:04,420
But you're not vegetarian, are you?

10
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:06,300
Well, not entirely.

11
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:12,180
I don't eat meat at all, but I don't have a problem eating fish, seafood, or eggs.

12
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:13,920
Isn't that very difficult?

13
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:18,560
I can't imagine giving up a premium juicy steak or bacon cheeseburgers.

14
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:19,820
I wouldn't know.

15
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:22,200
My parents followed the same diet.

16
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:25,640
Even when I was younger, we never had meat during meals.

17
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:28,640
And I never really had a craving to try it.

18
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:33,360
I bet it would be hard though, giving up something you're accustomed to eating.

19
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:37,560
I tried cutting back on coffee in the morning and it was awful.

20
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:43,860
Come to think of it, it was probably even worse for my colleagues that had to deal with me at morning meetings.

21
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:47,480
That's even harder to imagine you without coffee.

22
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:52,420
So you say your parents also followed a partially vegetarian diet.

23
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:54,500
Why did they decide to do that?

24
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:56,280
That's an interesting question.

25
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:58,640
I never thought to ask them though.

26
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:01,500
My best guess is for health reasons.

27
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:08,620
They're not terribly active or knowledgeable about animal preservation efforts, but they're serious about their health.

28
00:02:08,620 --> 00:02:16,040
Both are in their 70s now, eat organic as often as possible and take part in regular physical exercise.

29
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:20,260
Neither of them has any health problems, and they hardly ever get sick.

30
00:02:20,260 --> 00:02:23,500
In that case, I might give it a try someday.

31
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:24,920
Now back to the menu.

32
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:33,260
What are you going to have?

33
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:51,500
Q1. Where does the conversation most probably take place?

34
00:02:51,500 --> 00:03:08,200
Q2. What do we learn about the woman?

35
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:26,880
Q3. What would be a difficult thing to do according to the woman?

36
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:45,440
Q4. What does the woman say about her parents?

37
00:03:45,440 --> 00:03:49,700
CONVERSATION 2
How did your annual performance evaluation meeting with your manager go?

38
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:51,680
Did you get much in the way of praise?

39
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:53,160
Next to zero.

40
00:03:53,160 --> 00:04:00,640
When we came to the part about discussing my areas for growth, he bluntly told me that I had an attitude problem.

41
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:03,760
But he wouldn't really expand on that description.

42
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:06,600
He said it's the little things I do and say.

43
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:09,600
Did you ask him for a specific example?

44
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:13,960
I did ask him a few more questions to try to narrow it down.

45
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:19,920
He said my constant questioning of him and his decisions was the most obvious example he could give.

46
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:24,180
Then our conversation was swiftly brought to a close.

47
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:29,360
I had a similar experience once, my manager told me I was too honest.

48
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:36,460
In most cases, I thought my honesty was helpful, as did my colleagues, but my manager thought otherwise.

49
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:43,220
Some managers really adopt manipulative language to disguise the unreasonable choices they have made.

50
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:50,860
They don't use the rational power of arguments to resolve issues of conflict or complaints about unfairness.

51
00:04:50,860 --> 00:04:52,100
Right.

52
00:04:52,100 --> 00:05:02,640
Some employ their persuasive and influential communication style to win workplace arguments by compelling people to perform the intended action.

53
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:03,820
Exactly.

54
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:06,620
Too honest, isn't that a good thing?

55
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:13,280
I thought that's surely something we should have covered earlier in the section discussing strengths and talents.

56
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:24,240
But now, honesty was deemed to be more of a sin than a virtue, since it made people feel uncomfortable or at least it made the most powerful folks feel uncomfortable.

57
00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:32,440
Indeed, rhetoric is viewed by many philosophers as the method through which the powerful accumulate more power.

58
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:39,660
By virtue of having the loudest voice, they're able to command greater attention and to further assert their dominance.

59
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:43,480
Even when what they're saying doesn't really make much sense.

60
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:47,420
And retaining their power and prestige is their priority.

61
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:56,760
It's what makes the most sense to a lot of them.

62
00:05:56,760 --> 00:06:16,840
Q5. What was the manager's reason for giving little praise for the man's performance?

63
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:34,760
Q6. What does the woman say about some managers?

64
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:53,640
Q7. According to the woman, what do some managers think of honesty?

65
00:06:53,640 --> 00:07:39,780
Q8. What does the woman think is the priority of many managers?

66
00:07:39,780 --> 00:07:45,200
PASSAGE 1
Athletes are seen as heroes because they can do things that most of us can't do.

67
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:55,980
They can hit fast balls coming at them at nearly 100 miles an hour, and leap and hang in the air, seemingly defying gravity.

68
00:07:55,980 --> 00:08:05,720
They get paid millions of dollars for their efforts, and their names and faces appear on everything from running shoes to advertising boards.

69
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:15,220
Athletes who are champions also show qualities such as perseverance, dedication, and the ability to keep their cool under pressure.

70
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:19,920
Many show those same qualities off the playing field, too.

71
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:28,620
Stories about superstar athletes teach us about working hard and believing in ourselves and in being passionate about what we do.

72
00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:39,500
Although it's usually bad behavior that gets an athlete a spot on the 6 o'clock news, many high-profile players work hard to be positive role models to children.

73
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:51,900
They raise money for charities and act as mentors, talking to student groups and volunteering their time to programs that help children keep off drugs and stay in school.

74
00:08:51,900 --> 00:08:55,580
Still, even the greatest champions have flaws.

75
00:08:55,580 --> 00:09:03,700
Just because an athlete has the perfect golf swing doesn't mean he is the perfect parent, friend, or spokesperson.

76
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:06,280
They also make mistakes.

77
00:09:06,280 --> 00:09:11,600
Separating an athlete's professional and personal lives can be tough.

78
00:09:11,600 --> 00:09:20,240
When a sports star gets in trouble with the law or does something wrong in their private life, fans are often left disappointed.

79
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:38,680
Before he died, baseball star, Mickey Mantle, who was plagued with alcohol problems, told young ball players and the fans who admired him "To play like me; don't be like me".

80
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:56,720
Q9. Why does the speaker say athletes are often viewed as heroes?

81
00:09:56,720 --> 00:10:15,076
Q10. What does the speaker say many high-profile players do?

82
00:10:15,076 --> 00:10:31,500
Q11. What does the speaker say can be difficult?

83
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:36,440
PASSAGE 2
We don't need to tell you that weddings can get expensive.

84
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:42,800
Even with the most meticulous budgeting, a few unexpected costs are bound to occur.

85
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:52,160
While most brides tend to accept this as fact, one Canadian woman who is only known as Susan, attempted to avoid all wedding costs.

86
00:10:52,160 --> 00:10:57,300
She did this by asking her friends and family to pay up to attend her wedding.

87
00:10:57,300 --> 00:11:00,480
It went about as well as you'd expect.

88
00:11:00,480 --> 00:11:09,280
Susan is causing quite the debate online after posting a bizarre Facebook complaint about her now canceled wedding.

89
00:11:09,280 --> 00:11:25,020
Yes, the couple called off the wedding just days before their wedding vows, since the guests refused to pay the $1500 attendance fee, the $60000 dream wedding was put on permanent hold.

90
00:11:25,020 --> 00:11:34,620
In her long explanation filled with cursing and swearing, the bride accused her friends and family of ruining her marriage and her life.

91
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:41,360
She stated that each guest would only need to pay $1500, while she sacrificed everything for the day.

92
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:51,000
Her maid of honour told her to stick to the budget as she was asking too much from her guests, but Susan ignored her.

93
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:58,960
Not surprisingly, only 8 people responded positively to the wedding invitations and money requests.

94
00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:06,060
Realizing they would not be able to afford their dream wedding, Susan's future husband suggested getting married in Las Vegas.

95
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:13,800
The bride quickly shut down the idea, saying she did not want a wedding of gambling and heavy drinking.

96
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:24,792
It seems her dream wedding has now become a nightmare.

97
00:12:24,792 --> 00:12:42,380
Q12. What does the speaker say about weddings?

98
00:12:42,380 --> 00:13:01,260
Q13. What do we learn about the wedding of the Canadian woman, Susan?

99
00:13:01,260 --> 00:13:19,800
Q14. What did Susan's maid of honor advise her to do?

100
00:13:19,800 --> 00:14:04,700
Q15. How did Susan respond to her future husband's suggestion?

101
00:14:04,700 --> 00:14:10,960
RECORDING 1
It has long been scientifically established that weather changes can affect people's moods.

102
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:17,980
Now a new study has provided evidence that temperature can influence people's personalities.

103
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:19,760
This study of over 1.

104
00:14:19,760 --> 00:14:26,800
6 million people revealed that 22-degrees Celsius is the perfect air temperature to live in.

105
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:38,600
A city with an average annual temperature closer to 22 tends to have a population who are more agreeable, conscientious, emotionally stable, and outgoing.

106
00:14:38,600 --> 00:14:43,660
It is the least taxing temperature for the body to regulate its own temperature.

107
00:14:43,660 --> 00:14:54,620
The study was observational and didn't show cause and effect, but the scientists behind it theorized that better weather leads people to leave their home more often.

108
00:14:54,620 --> 00:15:03,680
This in turn leads to more social interaction, which encourages them to develop a friendlier and socially more acceptable personality.

109
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:07,900
Warmer climates also make people feel more positive in general.

110
00:15:07,900 --> 00:15:11,460
They tend to be more agreeable and conscientious.

111
00:15:11,460 --> 00:15:18,440
The findings might help explain why colder and warmer countries tend to produce people with different personalities.

112
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:30,040
Roughly speaking, about 40% of a person's personality is determined by their genes, the other 60% by their environment.

113
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:36,500
It was already well-known that personality traits vary across geographic regions.

114
00:15:36,500 --> 00:15:43,720
Scientists also knew that these geographic personality traits are associated with a broad range of consequential outcomes.

115
00:15:43,720 --> 00:15:56,840
These outcomes include economic activity, for example entrepreneurial startup rates, and also crime rates, health behaviors, and health outcomes.

116
00:15:56,840 --> 00:16:02,000
And it is well established that personality traits differ between countries.

117
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:06,580
The research team speculated the two might be linked.

118
00:16:06,580 --> 00:16:14,620
To test this, they gave online personality tests to 5587 Chinese students and 1.

119
00:16:14,620 --> 00:16:17,180
66 million Americans.

120
00:16:17,180 --> 00:16:22,540
They then compared the results with the average annual temperature where they grew up.

121
00:16:22,540 --> 00:16:28,060
The tests measured personality along 5 well-studied characteristics.

122
00:16:28,060 --> 00:16:38,140
The 5 were agreeability, conscientiousness, emotional stability, outgoingness, and openness to new experiences.

123
00:16:38,140 --> 00:16:51,080
In both groups, the researchers found the closer a town's average annual temperature was to 22 degrees, the more its population exhibited those personality characteristics.

124
00:16:51,080 --> 00:17:03,080
However, the findings were much stronger for the Chinese group than the Americans studied, which suggests that though temperature plays a role, it does not play a dominant role.

125
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:06,060
The effects are fairly weak.

126
00:17:06,060 --> 00:17:18,580
It's unlikely to lead to many arguments over the temperature setting of the office air conditioner.

127
00:17:18,580 --> 00:17:36,460
Q16. What has a new study revealed about temperature?

128
00:17:36,460 --> 00:17:53,320
Q17. What does the speaker say about warmer climates?

129
00:17:53,320 --> 00:18:11,240
Q18. What did the online personality tests suggest?

130
00:18:11,240 --> 00:18:15,940
RECORDING 2
Today we're talking about loneliness.

131
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:26,060
Loneliness and social isolation are growing public health concerns for people of all ages in the United States, from adolescence to the elderly.

132
00:18:26,060 --> 00:18:30,280
Public health experts are worried because loneliness seems to be on the rise.

133
00:18:30,280 --> 00:18:40,360
And studies have long found correlations between loneliness and an assortment of medical conditions that threaten health and longevity.

134
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:44,460
The problem of loneliness may be even greater than we thought.

135
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:53,100
A new national poll found that about a third of older Americans are lonely; and almost as many seniors feel isolated.

136
00:18:53,100 --> 00:19:07,420
This is a serious problem, as research shows that chronic loneliness in particular can impair older adults' memory and damage their physical and mental health.

137
00:19:07,420 --> 00:19:15,860
Chronic loneliness even impacts the life expectancy of seniors, increasing their risk of early mortality.

138
00:19:15,860 --> 00:19:19,500
Let's take a closer look at that poll now.

139
00:19:19,500 --> 00:19:30,540
More than a third of seniors in the poll said they felt lonely at least some of the time, and 27% said they sometimes or often felt isolated.

140
00:19:30,540 --> 00:19:35,220
This reflects how much time the seniors spent with others.

141
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:43,060
Almost 30% said they socialized with friends, family, or neighbors once a week or less.

142
00:19:43,060 --> 00:19:47,140
Women were more likely than men to report loneliness.

143
00:19:47,140 --> 00:19:49,020
But there is good news.

144
00:19:49,020 --> 00:19:51,580
It looks like loneliness can be reversed.

145
00:19:51,580 --> 00:19:56,040
But researchers are still trying to determine the best way to do so.

146
00:19:56,040 --> 00:19:57,820
Why is that?

147
00:19:57,820 --> 00:20:06,020
Resolving the problem of loneliness among seniors often isn't as simple as getting them together with others or moving them in with their children.

148
00:20:06,020 --> 00:20:15,780
In fact, the poll found that seniors who lived with their children were more likely to report feeling lonely than those who didn't.

149
00:20:15,780 --> 00:20:23,160
This may be because loneliness refers to the discrepancy between actual and desired relationships.

150
00:20:23,160 --> 00:20:33,320
So it's possible that someone who lives alone doesn't meet that definition, while someone in a house full of busy people does.

151
00:20:33,320 --> 00:20:34,900
How can we solve the problem?

152
00:20:34,900 --> 00:20:52,200
Well, the researchers assert that it's important to address each person's underlying cause of loneliness, whether it's the death of a spouse, medical problems, or social expectations that haven't been fulfilled.

153
00:20:52,200 --> 00:20:56,460
It's noteworthy that there is one general recommendation.

154
00:20:56,460 --> 00:21:08,840
While finding solutions for loneliness is highly personal, research suggests the best interventions are those that involve meaningful social contact at least once a week.

155
00:21:08,840 --> 00:21:23,360
Depending on the person, that could mean volunteering, calling an old friend, or something else.

156
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:44,320
Q19. Why does the speaker say the finding of a new national poll reveals a serious problem?

157
00:21:44,320 --> 00:22:02,140
Q20. What is the good news the speaker talks about?

158
00:22:02,140 --> 00:22:20,620
Q21. What is the key to solving the loneliness problem, according to the speaker?

159
00:22:20,620 --> 00:22:28,800
RECORDING 3
Hello, I am co-founder of the popular female travel community, We Are Travel Girls.

160
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:34,040
We collect and publish stories from women traveling all over the world.

161
00:22:34,040 --> 00:22:35,940
We promote women's blogs.

162
00:22:35,940 --> 00:22:44,820
We host meetings and events and are getting ready to launch our Travel with Us trips in Bali, Japan and Malawi.

163
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:55,720
Before I started We Are Travel Girls, I had a successful 10-year career in finance in London where I advised private clients on their investments.

164
00:22:55,720 --> 00:23:05,480
Having always had a huge love for travel, I finally took the leap and left finance to pursue my dream of starting a travel company.

165
00:23:05,480 --> 00:23:09,820
For as long as I can remember, I wanted to be a travel writer.

166
00:23:09,820 --> 00:23:20,420
Before blogs existed, and everything we read was online, I would go to travel writing seminars by writers who were published in travel magazines.

167
00:23:20,420 --> 00:23:26,860
I was desperate to write for one of those magazines but didn't know how to break into that industry.

168
00:23:26,860 --> 00:23:34,020
After university, I ended up working in finance, but always had a desire to travel and write about it.

169
00:23:34,020 --> 00:23:46,600
In 2015, I was looking at ways to leave finance, and my friend Vanessa, who grew up on a ranch on the central coast of California, suggested starting a blog.

170
00:23:46,600 --> 00:23:52,280
But when we started creating it, we realized that was the same thing every girl was doing.

171
00:23:52,280 --> 00:23:56,640
So we turned our attention to creating a community among these women.

172
00:23:56,640 --> 00:24:05,300
This led us to start We Are Travel Girls, which has now grown into a community of over 200,000 followers.

173
00:24:05,300 --> 00:24:14,040
To anyone thinking about becoming a travel writer, I would suggest they first try and look for a unique way to enter the industry.

174
00:24:14,040 --> 00:24:26,404
There are a lot of travel writers now and it can be hard to stand out from the crowd.

175
00:24:26,404 --> 00:24:34,213
My advice would be to find a niche and focus on that.

176
00:24:34,213 --> 00:24:40,720
For example, if you love adventure travel, focus on that.

177
00:24:40,720 --> 00:24:42,033
If you love food, focus on food travel.

178
00:24:42,033 --> 00:24:44,331
The more specific you are, the easier it will be to build an audience.

179
00:24:44,331 --> 00:24:46,465
Another thing I would suggest is to avoid too much advertising early on.

180
00:24:46,465 --> 00:24:49,256
When you first start out, your audience is there for your content, not to be sold to.

181
00:24:49,256 --> 00:24:51,390
If you start bombarding them with ads too early, they will lose interest.

182
00:24:51,390 --> 00:24:54,180
Wait until you have built up a loyal following before you start introducing any kind of advertising.

183
00:24:54,180 --> 00:25:14,620
Q22. What do we learn about the speaker before she started We Are Travel Girls?

184
00:25:14,620 --> 00:25:24,320
Q23. What did the speaker's friend Vanessa suggest she do in 2015?

185
00:25:24,320 --> 00:25:34,020
Q24. What does the speaker suggest people do to become travel writers?

186
00:25:34,020 --> 00:26:15,079
Q25. What does the speaker advise travel writers to do when they first start out?
